WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:05.350
There are plenty of reasons why an actor might
want to leave a show, even one that's as highly

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:10.880
rated as NCIS. From personal decisions to
behind-the-scenes drama, here are the real

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.660
reasons why these actors left the world of
NCIS.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:19.720
Michael Weatherly was one of the original
cast members of the flagship NCIS. But working

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:24.730
in one place for over a dozen years can be
a drag, even for an actor on a hit television

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:29.440
show. According to Weatherly, his decision
to leave the show back in 2016 was one of

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:35.260
boredom. He was, quote, "burnt out by NCIS"
and was tired of playing his character, Tony

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:39.500
DiNozzo, for 13 seasons. He told the Television
Critics Association:

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.260
"It really felt like a circle that had gone
all the way around, and I really felt happy

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:47.400
with the resolution of character and my time
on the show."

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:52.190
It seems Weatherly wasn't too tired of the
TV series grind in general, because a mere

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:57.100
two months after his departure from NCIS was
made public, it was announced that the actor

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:03.800
would be the star of CBS' Bull, a series inspired
by the early career of Dr. Phil McGraw. But

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:09.070
Weatherly's tenure on that show hasn't been
without controversy. In December 2018, CBS

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:15.970
paid former Bull co-star, Eliza Dushku, $9.5
million to settle a sexual harassment claim

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:20.890
against Weatherly. Dushku said she was written
out of the show after confronting the actor

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:26.140
about his behavior. Despite the big payout,
Bull is still currently in production at CBS.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:32.120
Pauley Perrette's decision to leave NCIS is
one wrapped up in a bit of mystery.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:37.740
"Okay, we have a lot to get through, so let's
get started. Number one, am I okay? Yes."

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.159
When she announced her departure in 2017,
she tweeted:

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:47.000
"It was a decision made last year. I hope
everyone will love and enjoy EVERYTHING ABBY

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.280
not only for the rest of this season but for
everything she has given all of us for 16

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:52.780
years."

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:59.140
Since then, Perrette has been clear about
why she'll never return to NCIS. In June 2019,

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:03.940
she tweeted that she wasn't ever coming back
to the show. Her reason? Well, according to

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:04.440
Perrette:

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:08.760
"I am terrified of Harmon and him attacking
me. I have nightmares about it."

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:15.400
The whole thing apparently stems from Mark
Harmon's dog biting an NCIS crew member, and

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:19.930
according to Perrette, quote, "a very powerful
publicity 'machine'" was trying to keep her

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:26.489
silent about what went down between her and
her co-star. As for CBS, the studio responded

00:02:26.489 --> 00:02:28.439
to Perrette's allegations saying:

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:33.600
"Over a year ago, Pauley came to us with a
workplace concern. We took the matter seriously

00:02:33.609 --> 00:02:37.949
and worked with her to find a resolution.
We are committed to a safe work environment

00:02:37.949 --> 00:02:39.209
on all our shows."

00:02:40.319 --> 00:02:45.249
Although not on the show from the start, Cote
de Pablo's character, Ziva David, was a favorite

00:02:45.249 --> 00:02:51.040
among the NCIS fanbase. She joined the show
in 2005 for season three but left abruptly

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:56.609
in 2013 in the beginning of season 11. She
was the first of the major characters to exit

00:02:56.609 --> 00:03:00.020
the hit series, and after her departure, she
told TV Guide:

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:05.079
"As far as my decision to leave, that's a
personal thing...It was an overwhelmingly

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.060
hard thing, at times terrifying."

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:11.950
A few years later, though, the actress said
her departure was because she felt the show

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:16.340
wasn't treating her character right. As she
explained in a 2016 Q&amp;A session:

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:22.740
"I worked eight years crafting this character
and loving her. And so when I felt or I perceived

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:26.930
that the character was not being treated with
the respect that she deserved, no money in

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:28.520
the world [would make me stay]."

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:35.060
De Pablo's Ziva did eventually return, however,
appearing in both seasons 16 and 17.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.600
When Sasha Alexander told producers she was
ready to leave NCIS after two seasons, they

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:42.980
sent off her character with a bang.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:48.379
Special Agent Kate Todd was shot in the head,
shocking fans of the show. But why did she

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:52.720
leave in such a grisly way? As it turns out,
she was just ready to move on.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.849
Executive producer Donald P. Bellisario decided
to give the character a dramatic send-off

00:03:57.849 --> 00:04:02.439
when Alexander expressed a desire to depart.
He told the Chicago Tribune:

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:07.320
"I had already started writing the last episode
of the season and was getting ready to go

00:04:07.329 --> 00:04:12.390
to Australia to take a little break. Sasha
came in two days before I was to leave, and

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:15.320
with tears in her eyes, she said, 'I just
can't work this hard.'"

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:18.340
After all, as the actress said to TV Guide
in 2012:

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:23.720
"People don't realize that on a network show,
you make 24 episodes a year, that's 10½ months

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.620
a year, 17 hours a day. It's hard core."

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:32.460
After leaving the show in 2005, Alexander
went on to star in Rizzoli &amp; Isles which ran

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:37.960
on TNT for seven seasons. She was also a recurring
character on Shameless and has appeared on

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:41.900
several other shows, including Law &amp; Order:
SVU and Curb Your Enthusiasm.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:48.060
Lauren Holly was a part of NCIS from seasons
three to five, where she played Jenny Shepard,

00:04:48.069 --> 00:04:53.009
the director in charge of the NCIS team. It
was just supposed to be a six-episode guest-starring

00:04:53.009 --> 00:04:59.090
arc, but Holly wound up playing the mysteriously
ill director about 48 episodes. She eventually

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:01.380
left because she was tired of playing the
character.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.580
The actress had been taking a break prior
to signing on with NCIS, so after three seasons,

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:07.240
she explained:

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:11.980
"To be honest, now that the work bug had bitten
me again, I got bored with my part of the

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:12.480
Director."

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:17.840
However, it wasn't illness that killed off
her character. Nope, Jenny Shepard was killed

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:22.940
by the fiance of a Russian crime lord that
she'd been sent to take out years earlier.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:36.060
Fortunately, Holly herself is alive and well,
and she's appeared in movies like The Blackcoat's

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:41.070
Daughter and Crank: High Voltage, as well
as a number of TV shows, including Designated

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:41.560
Survivor.

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:48.600
English-born Duane Henry joined NCIS in season
13, playing Clayton Reeves, a former British

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:54.500
MI-6 officer who joins the NCIS International
Desk. Sadly, Henry was only on the show for

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:58.939
two seasons before being killed off in the
same episode where Pauley Perrette makes her

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:00.320
final appearance as Abby.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:03.979
Henry says his exit was partly due to the
fact that the show didn't know what to do

00:06:03.979 --> 00:06:10.569
with his character after the death of former
NCIS showrunner Gary Glasberg. As Henry explained

00:06:10.569 --> 00:06:11.200
to TV Line:

00:06:11.569 --> 00:06:16.660
"Gary was obviously 'my guy.'...Clayton was
Gary's brainchild, he created this character.

00:06:16.669 --> 00:06:21.189
So when Gary passed, I was feeling a bit of
panic in people's faces. I could tell that

00:06:21.189 --> 00:06:23.400
they were not really sure [what the future
held]."

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:27.659
As a result, Henry knew the end could be near
for his character, and he prepared himself

00:06:27.659 --> 00:06:32.719
for his departure. When asked if he was upset
about the way Clayton actually went out, the

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:33.220
actor said:

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:38.280
"This is TV history at its finest. You can't
ask for a better exit. Like, come on, man."

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:42.660
Although Henry's exit from the show was completely
amicable, the fact that his character was

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:46.820
killed means we've definitely seen the last
of Clayton Reeves on NCIS.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:52.860
Jennifer Esposito might have only been on
a single season of NCIS, but she definitely

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:58.870
made an impact with fans as Special Agent
Alexandra Quinn. Prior to joining NCIS, Quinn

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:04.219
had spent 15 years training agents, including
Bishop, Torres, and McGee. So fans were surprised

00:07:04.219 --> 00:07:08.219
when it was revealed Esposito wouldn't be
returning to the hit show. There was some

00:07:08.219 --> 00:07:12.259
speculation that the actress might've been
ill. At least that's what Esposito had been

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:15.319
hearing when she addressed the rumors on Twitter
herself saying:

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:21.560
"No, I am not ill, but I so appreciate your
concern. Was not meant to be there long but

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:25.080
happy I was. Met some great new friends! Love
2u all."

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:29.960
According to Esposito, Quinn wasn't going
to be a long-term character, and her departure

00:07:29.969 --> 00:07:34.840
from NCIS wasn't as surprising as it seemed.
However, there's always a chance that Quinn

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:38.870
could return at some point, as she'd merely
left the team to care for her mother, who'd

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:41.120
been diagnosed with Alzheimer's disease.

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:47.240
One of the show's most mysterious departures
was Zoe McLellan's exit from NCIS: New Orleans.

00:07:47.249 --> 00:07:52.759
At the time, producers called it a "creative
decision," but a 2019 article by Vulture reveals

00:07:52.759 --> 00:07:57.699
an entirely different reason for McLellan's
character, Special Agent Meredith Brody, being

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:00.060
written out of the show: She was fired.

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:05.060
According to the publication, NCIS: New Orleans'
original showrunner, Jeffrey Lieber, said

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:09.811
he was under tremendous pressure to, quote,
"get on board" with firing McLellan. Lieber

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:14.920
wasn't told why his higher-ups wanted her
gone, and said he fought back, explaining:

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:19.400
"I thought she was doing a good job and that
the audience was connected to her, especially

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:21.120
because testing bore that out."

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:26.220
So what was the real reason the CBS brass
wanted her gone? As Lieber explained:

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:31.340
"Finally, one powerful man putting pressure
on me just admitted to me that the problem

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:36.880
was that Les didn't find her 'f--able' enough,
and that he had felt that way from day one."

00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:43.720
Les is, of course, Les Moonves, the former
CBS chairman who resigned in 2018 after multiple

00:08:43.729 --> 00:08:48.240
sexual harassment allegations were brought
against him. After Lieber's departure from

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:53.440
the show, Brad Kern took over as showrunner
and proceeded to talk poorly about McLellan.

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:58.220
Kern apparently told his staff he didn't,
quote, "find her sexually attractive."

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:04.320
McLellan has refused to comment on any of
these revelations. In 2018, Kern stepped down

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:09.089
as showrunner but remained as a consulting
producer. After an outside party was brought

00:09:09.089 --> 00:09:14.140
in to investigate a third charge of sexual
harassment, Kern was finally fired from the

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:16.460
show, and his deal with CBS was terminated.

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:22.800
Shalita Grant's departure from NCIS: New Orleans
seemed to come as a shock to fans of the show.

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:29.000
She was originally hired as a recurring character,
ATF Agent Sonja Percy, and she became a full-time

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:34.399
cast member as an NCIS special agent in season
two. Grant was with the show through most

00:09:34.399 --> 00:09:38.940
of season four, but there isn't much info
about the specific reasons why she left the

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:39.440
series.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:44.260
According to Deadline, the actress said her
decision to leave the show was, quote, "mutually

00:09:44.279 --> 00:09:48.980
agreed upon." Variety reported that the split
from the show was "amicable," and in the same

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:50.540
article, Grant's manager said:

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:52.500
"It's just time for a change."

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:58.320
Fans of NCIS: New Orleans were absolutely
stunned when Special Agent Christopher LaSalle

00:09:58.339 --> 00:10:03.500
was shot and killed in the middle of season
six. After being shot in the chest, it looked

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:08.019
as though the beloved character would pull
through, but instead, he died in the hospital

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:09.780
in front of his shocked team.

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:11.060
"That's enough."

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:16.440
"No, no, no, please, there's gotta be something
you can do."

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:17.340
"Time."

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:17.840
"7:02."

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:22.440
It was a tragic moment for the characters
on the show, and it was also pretty heartbreaking

00:10:22.449 --> 00:10:26.870
for fans at home. After all, actor Lucas Black
had been with the series from day one, and

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:32.380
his Southern drawl and down-home charm endeared
him to viewers. Black posted a video on Instagram

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:37.540
for fans of NCIS: New Orleans to watch after
his last episode aired, where he told fans:

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:41.080
"Well, that's all she wrote for Agent LaSalle."

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:43.540
And he went on to say:

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:49.020
"I wanted to take this time personally to
thank all of you fans out there for your love

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:50.020
and support."

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:55.440
So, why exactly did Black leave the show after
six seasons? According to the actor, he felt

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:59.940
he needed to put his focus on his family.
In a video posted on Deadline, Black said

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:02.279
that working on the show wasn't easy for him:

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:08.160
"There's a lot of priorities in my life that
get sacrificed for me to be here, but it's

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:12.380
time for me to focus on those priorities in
my life."

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:19.279
Miguel Ferrer played NCIS Assistant Director
Owen Granger on NCIS: Los Angeles from its

00:11:19.279 --> 00:11:26.050
beginning in 2009 until his untimely death
in 2017. Sadly, Ferrer died from throat cancer

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:31.320
at the age of 61, and his condition was written
into the show by having Granger suffer from

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:32.620
an unnamed disease.

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:37.170
Although frail towards the end of his life,
Ferrer appeared on the show for as long as

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:42.320
he could. Chris O'Donnell, Ferrer's long time
co-star, thinks that he hung on longer than

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:46.639
he might have because Ferrer looked forward
to coming to work. As he told CBS:

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:51.319
"I think the show was kind of keeping him
alive for a while there because it gave him

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:52.600
something to look forward to."

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:57.550
After Ferrer passed away, the show paid tribute
to the actor with an "In Memoriam" in the

00:11:57.550 --> 00:12:02.290
closing credits, as well as playing a version
of "Knocking on Heaven's Door" recorded by

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:04.660
Ferrer and his band, the Jenerators.

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:09.260
